    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ce8eceac-02eb-56da-a8ca-1fae435431ff",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-4$ and $Q(x)=x{e}^{4x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-4x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y) & ={e}^{-4x}\\cdot x{e}^{4x} \\\\ {e}^{-4x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4{e}^{-4x}\\cdot y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-4x}) & =x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-4x})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{-4x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}{2}+C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
